menu "Baseband Guard"

config BBG
	bool "Baseband Guard block device write protection"
	depends on SECURITY && SECURITY_SELINUX && BLOCK
	default n
	help
	  Deny writes, attribute changes and destructive ioctls on block
	  devices that are not on a small allowlist, for processes that
	  descend from an execve into an su, ksu or magisk SELinux domain.

	  The allowlist covers the partitions a root user has a legitimate
	  reason to rewrite - boot, dtbo, the vbmeta set, userdata,
	  metadata, misc and cache - plus zram.  Everything else, including
	  preloader, lk, tee, the modem images and the raw disks, becomes
	  read-only for root.  Processes that never exec through a root
	  domain, such as update_engine or a vendor HAL, are unaffected.

	  This requires "baseband_guard" in CONFIG_LSM, listed after
	  "selinux" so that SELinux keeps cred blob offset 0.  The build
	  fails with an explicit error if it is missing.

	  This is a guard against destructive mistakes, not a privilege
	  boundary: anything able to load a module or write kernel memory
	  can switch it off.

	  If unsure, say N.

config BBG_BLOCK_BOOT
	bool "Also protect boot, init_boot, vendor_boot and vendor_kernel_boot"
	depends on BBG
	default n
	help
	  Remove the boot images from the allowlist.  Flashing a kernel
	  from a running Android system then stops working; recovery,
	  fastbootd and the bootloader remain the only way in.

	  If unsure, say N.

config BBG_BLOCK_RECOVERY
	bool "Also protect recovery"
	depends on BBG
	default n
	help
	  Remove the recovery partition from the allowlist.  Flashing a
	  custom recovery from a running Android system then stops working.

	  If unsure, say N.

endmenu
